\title{Where the CI time and the cache space go}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-14}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{tooling}
\tag{ci}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: the job logs and the cache bucket on 2026-11-14, one cold run of \pr{1012} and the runs on the main branch after it. The numbers are from the ARM Linux job unless stated.}
\ul{
\li{\strong{Bazel's compilations are cached, Bazel's work is not.} With a warm compiler cache the job had 4,988 hits and 18 misses, so the objects of the IFRT build are reused. Bazel itself keeps nothing between jobs: it fetches its dependencies and runs all 6,372 actions again, 48 minutes cold and 7 minutes when every compilation is a hit. Each later CMake target that depends on the archives starts it again for 10 to 20 seconds, seven more times in a job.}
\li{\strong{Bazel is not what fills the bucket.} Its objects are small; all objects under 1 MiB are 1.4 GiB over four platforms. Half of the 9.5 GiB of compiler outputs is in 75 objects.}
\li{\strong{The large objects are rewritten per commit.} One commit that changed C++ added 187 MiB to one platform: two C++ module interfaces, 69 and 34 MiB compressed, and \code{\verb~|reussir_backend_sys~} twice, 38 MiB each. \code{\verb~|mlir-sys~} is 130 MiB and was stored three to four times per job. Nothing in the workflows deletes objects, so the bucket is bounded only by its lifecycle rule.}
\li{\strong{A lifecycle rule expires by age, not by use.} An object that is still hit every day is deleted when it reaches the age limit, so the third-party crates and the IFRT objects, written together, expire together, and the run after that is cold.}
\li{\strong{Cargo rebuilt the same crates per invocation.} CMake calls cargo once per package, and cargo resolved features for each selection separately. Over the 18 build and test invocations that gave 775 library builds for 467 crates. In a job where every compilation was a hit, the test target of \code{\verb~|reussir-codegen~} still built for 8 minutes. See [dec-H84J](dec-H84J).}
\li{\strong{RUSTFLAGS decide whether a target directory is reused.} The same directory built once with \code{\verb~|RUSTFLAGS=-Awarnings~} and once with the flags of \code{\verb~|.cargo/config.toml~} compiles everything twice; this happened to the PJRT runtime, 196 crates.}
\li{\strong{A warm Linux job takes 55 minutes.} About 9 are Bazel, 11 are cargo steps that had no compiler cache, 8 the rebuild in the code generator's test target, and 10 are tests running.}
\li{\strong{The directory caches were uploaded on every run.} Their keys hashed every \code{\verb~|Cargo.lock~} under the checkout. After a build that includes lock files in the target directory, so the save step computed another key than the restore step, the exact key never hit, and each run uploaded the archive again, 1.3 GiB for the Windows test job. Fixed by \pr{1018}.}
\li{\strong{sccache runs the compiler without \code{\verb~|SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH~}.} The Nix shell sets the variable and clang honours it, but sccache 0.19 removes it from the environment of the compilations it runs. A C file that uses \code{\verb~|__DATE__~} or \code{\verb~|__TIME__~} then differs on every build and never hits the cache. mimalloc is such a file, and \code{\verb~|reussir-rt~} links it, so a run that changed no source still recompiled the runtime in every configuration: 13 to 15 Rust misses per Linux job. \pr{1020} gives the two macros fixed values.}
\li{\strong{Identical commits were tested more than once.} The merge queue tests the commit that becomes the head of the main branch, and the push then ran the same workflows on it. See [dec-E5LF](dec-E5LF).}
}
